Chapter: TlsCert
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The two handshake messages that turn an anonymous key agreement into an
 authenticated one: Certificate (RFC 8446 section 4.4.2) and
 CertificateVerify (section 4.4.3). Without these, DTLS agrees a key with
 whoever answered -- which is exactly what an active man in the middle
 arranges to be.

 CertificateVerify is the whole mechanism, and it is worth saying plainly
 what it does. The peer signs the TRANSCRIPT -- every handshake byte so far,
 including its own key share. So a man in the middle who substitutes his
 own key share changes the transcript, and the signature he would need is
 one over a transcript he cannot make the real peer sign. He does not hold
 the certificate's private key, so he cannot produce it. That is the entire
 defence, and it rests on the signature covering the transcript rather than
 covering, say, the certificate.

 THE SIGNED CONTENT IS NOT THE TRANSCRIPT HASH. RFC 8446 section 4.4.3
 wraps it:

   64 bytes of 0x20, then a context string, then one 0x00, then the hash.

 The 64 spaces exist so that a signature made by a TLS 1.3 peer can never
 be mistaken for one made by a TLS 1.2 peer, whose signed content began
 with the handshake hash directly. The context string differs by ROLE --
 "server CertificateVerify" versus "client CertificateVerify" -- so a
 server's signature cannot be replayed back at a client as if the client
 had made it. Drop the prefix, or use one role's string for both, and the
 message still verifies against itself while losing the property it exists
 for.

 The context string is ASCII. It goes through tls13-ascii and NOT through
 char-code, because Codex Text is CCE: char-code "TLS" gives the CCE code
 points, not 54 4c 53. This exact bug encoded every label in the TLS key
 schedule and shipped an SNI hostname no server could match (BACKLOG 5.8).
 It is invisible to a round-trip test, because both sides make the same
 mistake and agree.

 To tls-cv-content gives List Integer, building the bytes a CertificateVerify signs.
 To tls-cv-verify gives Boolean, checking a peer's CertificateVerify against its certificate's key.

 We say:

Section: Signature Schemes

 ed25519, 0x0807 (RFC 8446 section 4.2.3). Two bytes on the wire.

  tls-scheme-ed25519-hi : Integer = #08
  tls-scheme-ed25519-lo : Integer = #07

 signature_algorithms, extension type 13. A ClientHello that omits it is
 telling the server it cannot do certificate authentication at all, which
 is why the anonymous handshake never needed it.

  tls-ext-signature-algorithms : Integer = #0D

 The extension body: a two-byte list length, then the schemes. We offer
 exactly one, because we implement exactly one, and offering a scheme we
 cannot perform is how a handshake dies late instead of early.

  tls-sigalgs-ext : List Integer =
    [#00, tls-ext-signature-algorithms, #00, #04, #00, #02,
     tls-scheme-ed25519-hi, tls-scheme-ed25519-lo]

Section: The Signed Content

 64 spaces, the role's context string, a zero, and the transcript hash.

  tls-cv-space-count : Integer = 64

  tls-cv-content : Boolean, List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cv-content (is-server) (transcript-hash) =
    tls-cv-spaces 0 [] & tls-cv-context is-server & [#00] & transcript-hash

  tls-cv-spaces : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cv-spaces (i) (acc) =
    if i >= tls-cv-space-count then acc
    else tls-cv-spaces (i + 1) (list-push acc #20)

 The role separation. These two strings are the reason a server's signature
 cannot be reflected at a client.

  tls-cv-context : Boolean -> List Integer
  tls-cv-context (is-server) =
    if is-server then tls13-ascii "TLS 1.3, server CertificateVerify"
    else tls13-ascii "TLS 1.3, client CertificateVerify"

Section: CertificateVerify

 struct { SignatureScheme algorithm; opaque signature<0..2^16-1>; }

  tls-cv-encode : Boolean, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cv-encode (is-server) (priv) (pub) (transcript-hash) =
    tls-cv-wrap (ed25519-sign priv pub (tls-cv-content is-server transcript-hash))

  tls-cv-wrap : List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cv-wrap (sig) =
    [tls-scheme-ed25519-hi, tls-scheme-ed25519-lo,
     bit-shru (list-length sig) 8, bit-and (list-length sig) #FF] & sig

 Checking one. The key comes from the peer's certificate -- which the chain
 walk has already decided to trust -- and it must be a key that can sign at
 all, which X509 makes a type rather than a hope.

 is-server is what the CHECKER expects, not what the message claims. A
 message does not get to tell us which role it was signed for; that is the
 whole point of the context string.

  tls-cv-verify : Boolean, X509Key, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  tls-cv-verify (expect-server) (key) (body) (transcript-hash) =
    if x509-can-verify key == False then False
    else if list-length body < 4 then False
    else tls-cv-scheme expect-server key body transcript-hash

 An unexpected scheme is refused rather than ignored. A verifier that skips
 the algorithm field is a verifier that can be handed a signature of a kind
 it did not agree to.

  tls-cv-scheme : Boolean, X509Key, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  tls-cv-scheme (expect-server) (key) (body) (transcript-hash) =
    if list-at body 0 /= tls-scheme-ed25519-hi then False
    else if list-at body 1 /= tls-scheme-ed25519-lo then False
    else tls-cv-sig expect-server key body transcript-hash (tls-cv-len body)

  tls-cv-len : List Integer -> Integer
  tls-cv-len (body) = list-at body 2 * 256 + list-at body 3

 The declared length must match what is actually there. A length field
 nobody checks is a length field an attacker writes.

  tls-cv-sig : Boolean, X509Key, List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  tls-cv-sig (expect-server) (key) (body) (transcript-hash) (n) =
    if n /= 64 then False
    else if list-length body /= 4 + n then False
    else ed25519-verify (key.xk-bytes) (tls-cv-content expect-server transcript-hash) (tlsc-slice body 4 n)

Section: Certificate

 struct { opaque certificate_request_context<0..2^8-1>;
          CertificateEntry certificate_list<0..2^24-1>; }
 CertificateEntry { opaque cert_data<1..2^24-1>;
                    Extension extensions<0..2^16-1>; }

 The request context is empty for a server's certificate. Three-byte
 lengths throughout, which is the part everyone gets wrong once.

  tls-cert-encode : List (List Integer) -> List Integer
  tls-cert-encode (certs) = tls-cert-wrap (tls-cert-entries certs 0 [])

  tls-cert-wrap : List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cert-wrap (entries) = [#00] & tls-u24 (list-length entries) & entries

  tls-cert-entries : List (List Integer), Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cert-entries (certs) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length certs then acc
    else tls-cert-entries certs (i + 1) (acc & tls-cert-entry (list-at certs i))

 Each entry: the DER, then an empty extensions vector.

  tls-cert-entry : List Integer -> List Integer
  tls-cert-entry (der) = tls-u24 (list-length der) & der & [#00, #00]

  tls-u24 : Integer -> List Integer
  tls-u24 (n) = [bit-and (bit-shru n 16) #FF, bit-and (bit-shru n 8) #FF, bit-and n #FF]

  tls-u24-at : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  tls-u24-at (bs) (i) =
    list-at bs i * 65536 + list-at bs (i + 1) * 256 + list-at bs (i + 2)

 Parsing one. Every length is checked against what is actually present
 before it is believed, because a Certificate message is the first thing an
 unauthenticated peer sends us.

  tls-cert-parse : List Integer -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-parse (body) =
    if list-length body < 4 then None
    else tls-cert-ctx body (list-at body 0)

  tls-cert-ctx : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-ctx (body) (ctx-len) =
    if 1 + ctx-len + 3 > list-length body then None
    else tls-cert-list body (1 + ctx-len)

  tls-cert-list : List Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-list (body) (off) =
    if off + 3 > list-length body then None
    else tls-cert-list-of body (off + 3) (tls-u24-at body off)

  tls-cert-list-of : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-list-of (body) (off) (n) =
    if off + n /= list-length body then None
    else tls-cert-walk body off (off + n) []

 The walk stops on the first thing it cannot read. It does not skip ahead
 looking for something that parses.

  tls-cert-walk : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List (List Integer) -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-walk (body) (off) (stop) (acc) =
    if off == stop then Just acc
    else if off + 3 > stop then None
    else tls-cert-one body off stop acc (tls-u24-at body off)

  tls-cert-one : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List (List Integer), Integer -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-one (body) (off) (stop) (acc) (n) =
    if n < 1 then None
    else if off + 3 + n + 2 > stop then None
    else tls-cert-ext body (off + 3 + n) stop (list-push acc (tlsc-slice body (off + 3) n))

  tls-cert-ext : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List (List Integer) -> Maybe (List (List Integer))
  tls-cert-ext (body) (off) (stop) (acc) =
    if off + 2 > stop then None
    else tls-cert-walk body (off + 2 + (list-at body off * 256 + list-at body (off + 1))) stop acc

Section: Slice

  tlsc-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  tlsc-slice (bs) (off) (n) = tlsc-slice-loop bs off n 0 []

  tlsc-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  tlsc-slice-loop (bs) (off) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else tlsc-slice-loop bs off n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))
